(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','FITNESS CENTRE — From Monday the pool closes to lap swimmers at 7:00 p.m., and the last entry is 6:30 p.m. Aqua-fit classes continue until 8:30 p.m. in the shallow end.','NONE','["Lap swimming will end earlier than the classes do.","The pool now closes all day.","Aqua-fit classes have been cancelled.","Entry is free after 6:30 p.m."]','A',1,'The pool shuts to lap swimmers at 7:00 p.m. while classes continue to 8:30 p.m., so lap swimming ends earlier than the classes do.',16),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Mei: "Bin day moved to Thursday, so leave the green bag out tonight." — Sam: "Doing it now before I forget."','NONE','["Change the collection day.","Put a bin bag out tonight.","Buy fresh bin bags.","Wait until Thursday to decide."]','B',1,'Sam agrees to take the green bag out tonight because the collection day moved to Thursday.',17),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','CAMPUS SECURITY — Your blue water bottle found in Lecture Hall 4 is now at the lost-and-found desk. Bring photo ID; items unclaimed after 14 days are donated.','NONE','["Show the original receipt.","Return a filled bottle.","Bring photo identification.","Sign a donation form."]','C',1,'The email tells the student to bring photo ID to collect the found bottle at the desk.',18),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','CAFE NOTICE — Guest wifi is free for one hour per visit; ask staff for the code at the till. Extending a session beyond that hour costs $2.','NONE','["Every session costs $2.","Staff keep the code secret.","Sessions never expire.","The free hour is limited to one session per visit."]','D',1,'Guest wifi is free for one hour per visit, so the free time is limited to a single session.',19),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the form instructions and answer the question.','LIBRARY CARD FORM — Print your full name and current address in capitals; staff verify your address against your student record before issuing the card.','NONE','["Staff compare the address with the student record.","The applicant pays a fee first.","Staff rewrite the form for the applicant.","Capital letters are optional."]','A',1,'Before issuing the card, staff verify the printed address against the student record.',20),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the timetable and answer the question.','ROUTE 12 — Buses run every 15 minutes before 9:00 a.m., every 10 minutes during the morning peak, and every 30 minutes after 8:00 p.m.','NONE','["Before 9:00 a.m.","During the morning peak.","After 8:00 p.m.","The wait never varies."]','B',1,'The morning peak gives a bus every 10 minutes, the shortest gap compared with 15 or 30 minutes.',21),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','FILM CLUB — We screen one foreign-language film each Friday with English subtitles. Voting for next month''s choice is open to members only.','NONE','["Any student on campus","The subtitle editors","Members of the club","The cinema staff"]','C',1,'Only members may vote for the next selection, so the choice belongs to members of the club.',22),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','DORMITORY — The Hall C elevator will be stopped for maintenance on Sunday from 1:00 to 4:00 p.m. Use the stairs or the Hall B elevator.','NONE','["Move out for the day.","Ride the Hall C elevator at 3:00.","All elevators will be shut.","Use the stairs or the Hall B elevator."]','D',1,'Because the Hall C elevator is stopped Sunday afternoon, residents are advised to use the stairs or the Hall B elevator.',23),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Leo: "Our meeting pushed to 6:00 because the room is booked at 5:00." — Nina: "I will send the new time to the group chat."','NONE','["Send the new meeting time to the group.","Book another room.","Cancel the meeting.","Arrive at 5:00 anyway."]','A',1,'Nina will post the moved 6:00 time in the group chat.',24),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','REGISTRAR — Your enrolment is confirmed, but one course still shows a prerequisite hold. Resend your transcript to clear it within five business days.','NONE','["Pay an enrolment fine.","Email the transcript again.","Drop the course.","Wait a month doing nothing."]','B',1,'The prerequisite hold is cleared by resending the transcript within five business days.',25),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','BIOLOGY LAB — Long hair must be tied back and closed shoes worn at all times. Anyone not dressed correctly will be asked to leave.','NONE','["A student wearing sandals.","A student with loose hair.","A student with tied hair and closed shoes.","A visitor in open shoes."]','C',1,'Only someone with hair tied back and closed shoes may remain in the lab.',26),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','PARKING OFFICE — Meter rates rise to $2 per hour from April 1. The first 15 minutes stay free. Permit holders use lots G and H only.','NONE','["The hourly rate.","The permitted lots.","The number of meters.","The free first 15 minutes."]','D',1,'Rates rise but the first 15 minutes stay free, so that free window remains unchanged.',27),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the schedule and answer the question.','STUDENT CLINIC — Walk-ins are seen from 8:00 to 10:00 a.m. Appointments fill the rest of the day. The clinic closes at 5:00 p.m.','NONE','["Between 8:00 and 10:00 a.m.","Any time in the afternoon.","After 5:00 p.m.","Only with a booking."]','A',1,'Walk-ins are seen from 8:00 to 10:00 a.m., while the rest of the day is by appointment.',28),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','BEACH CLEAN-UP — Meet at the north gate at 9:00 a.m. on Saturday. Gloves and bags are provided; bring water and a hat. Volunteers receive a service certificate.','NONE','["Gloves and bags.","Water and a hat.","A service certificate.","A packed lunch."]','B',1,'Gloves and bags are supplied, but volunteers must bring water and a hat.',29),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Tom: "I can leave at 7:15 instead of 7:30 if you want a ride." — Amy: "That works; I will wait at the library steps."','NONE','["At Tom''s home.","In the campus car park.","At the library steps.","At the bus station."]','C',1,'Amy says she will wait at the library steps for the earlier ride.',30),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','HOUSING OFFICE — Your room assignment is ready. Keys are collected at the front desk between 1:00 and 3:00 p.m. from Monday; the desk is unstaffed at lunchtime.','NONE','["Any time on Sunday.","At the desk during lunch.","Immediately today.","From Monday, between 1:00 and 3:00 p.m."]','D',1,'Keys are collected from Monday, 1:00 to 3:00 p.m., when the desk is staffed.',31),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','CAFE — The servery will close at 4:00 p.m. this Thursday for a staff meeting. Vending machines on the ground floor will stay open as normal.','NONE','["Snacks from the vending machines.","Full servery service.","A staff meal.","The open till counter."]','A',1,'The servery closes at 4:00 p.m. Thursday, but the ground-floor vending machines stay open.',32),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign-up form and answer the question.','RESEARCH SIGN-UP — This study needs left-handed students aged 18 to 25. Complete the short form and email it to the lab; places are filled in the order received.','NONE','["A right-handed teacher.","A 20-year-old left-handed student.","A 30-year-old left-handed adult.","Any lab visitor."]','B',1,'The study seeks left-handed students aged 18 to 25, so a 20-year-old left-handed student qualifies.',33),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the timetable and answer the question.','LIBRARY — Open 8:00 a.m. to 11:00 p.m. Monday to Thursday, to 6:00 p.m. on Friday, and noon to 6:00 p.m. on Sunday; closed Saturday.','NONE','["Sunday.","Friday.","Saturday.","Thursday."]','C',1,'The notice states that the library is closed on Saturday.',34),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Dan: "Tennis practice is moved indoors to Court 6 because of the rain." — Kim: "I will put the change on the club board."','NONE','["Because Court 6 was free.","Because the club closed.","Because Kim requested it.","Because the rain forced it indoors."]','D',1,'Practice is moved indoors to Court 6 because of the rain.',35),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the passage mainly about?','Until the late twentieth century, motor fuel was laced with tetraethyl lead, a compound added because it silenced engine knock and let engines run at higher, more efficient compression. The cost was invisible. Burning lead-treated fuel released fine particles that settled along roadsides and entered the air people breathed, and once inside the body lead accumulates, damaging the nervous system, with children the most vulnerable. Measuring lead in children''s blood in major cities showed worrying levels, and the figures fell sharply wherever regulators removed lead from petrol. Refineries resisted, warning that unleaded fuel would damage newer engines, until catalytic converters, which are poisoned by lead, made unleaded fuel technically necessary rather than merely desirable. Most countries had banned leaded petrol by the year 2000, and the decline in childhood blood-lead levels within a few years is taken as one of the clearest public-health gains from a single regulation.','NONE','["Why governments eventually banned leaded petrol and the effect of doing so.","How catalytic converters are manufactured.","The economics of oil refineries.","Why engines need higher compression."]','A',1,'The passage traces the reason leaded petrol was used, the health evidence against it, and the fall in blood-lead after the ban.',36),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "accumulates" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Until the late twentieth century, motor fuel was laced with tetraethyl lead, a compound added because it silenced engine knock and let engines run at higher, more efficient compression. The cost was invisible. Burning lead-treated fuel released fine particles that settled along roadsides and entered the air people breathed, and once inside the body lead accumulates, damaging the nervous system, with children the most vulnerable. Measuring lead in children''s blood in major cities showed worrying levels, and the figures fell sharply wherever regulators removed lead from petrol. Refineries resisted, warning that unleaded fuel would damage newer engines, until catalytic converters, which are poisoned by lead, made unleaded fuel technically necessary rather than merely desirable. Most countries had banned leaded petrol by the year 2000, and the decline in childhood blood-lead levels within a few years is taken as one of the clearest public-health gains from a single regulation.','NONE','["is measured","builds up","disappears","spreads evenly"]','B',1,'Lead gathers in the body over time, so "accumulates" means builds up.',37),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about catalytic converters?','Until the late twentieth century, motor fuel was laced with tetraethyl lead, a compound added because it silenced engine knock and let engines run at higher, more efficient compression. The cost was invisible. Burning lead-treated fuel released fine particles that settled along roadsides and entered the air people breathed, and once inside the body lead accumulates, damaging the nervous system, with children the most vulnerable. Measuring lead in children''s blood in major cities showed worrying levels, and the figures fell sharply wherever regulators removed lead from petrol. Refineries resisted, warning that unleaded fuel would damage newer engines, until catalytic converters, which are poisoned by lead, made unleaded fuel technically necessary rather than merely desirable. Most countries had banned leaded petrol by the year 2000, and the decline in childhood blood-lead levels within a few years is taken as one of the clearest public-health gains from a single regulation.','NONE','["They raised children''s blood-lead levels.","They made petrol cost more.","They could not function properly on leaded fuel.","They were invented only after the ban."]','C',1,'The passage says converters are poisoned by lead, so they could not work properly on leaded fuel.',38),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, what was the Antikythera device used to do?','Recovered from a shipwreck off the Greek island of Antikythera, a corroded bronze assembly turned out to contain more than thirty interlocking gears set within wooden frames. X-ray study of the fragments revealed the purpose: the device modelled the movements of the Sun and Moon and predicted when eclipses would occur, and a separate dial tracked the four-year cycle of Greek athletic games. The precision of the toothed wheels rivals mechanisms not built again for a thousand years, and the craftsmen evidently solved the difficult problem of representing the Moon''s uneven speed with a system of sliding pins. Because no comparable instrument survives from antiquity, researchers disagree on where the design was first made and how widely such devices were used. What is certain is that ancient Greek engineers combined astronomical theory and geared machinery far earlier than modern textbooks long assumed.','NONE','["To measure the depth of the wreck","To navigate ships at sea","To teach geometry to craftsmen","To predict eclipses and track astronomical cycles"]','D',1,'The gears modelled the Sun and Moon and forecast eclipses, so the device tracked astronomical cycles.',39),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "rivals" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Recovered from a shipwreck off the Greek island of Antikythera, a corroded bronze assembly turned out to contain more than thirty interlocking gears set within wooden frames. X-ray study of the fragments revealed the purpose: the device modelled the movements of the Sun and Moon and predicted when eclipses would occur, and a separate dial tracked the four-year cycle of Greek athletic games. The precision of the toothed wheels rivals mechanisms not built again for a thousand years, and the craftsmen evidently solved the difficult problem of representing the Moon''s uneven speed with a system of sliding pins. Because no comparable instrument survives from antiquity, researchers disagree on where the design was first made and how widely such devices were used. What is certain is that ancient Greek engineers combined astronomical theory and geared machinery far earlier than modern textbooks long assumed.','NONE','["matches","exceeds","copies","predates"]','A',1,'Here the precision equals that of later mechanisms, so "rivals" means matches.',40),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about other ancient geared devices?','Recovered from a shipwreck off the Greek island of Antikythera, a corroded bronze assembly turned out to contain more than thirty interlocking gears set within wooden frames. X-ray study of the fragments revealed the purpose: the device modelled the movements of the Sun and Moon and predicted when eclipses would occur, and a separate dial tracked the four-year cycle of Greek athletic games. The precision of the toothed wheels rivals mechanisms not built again for a thousand years, and the craftsmen evidently solved the difficult problem of representing the Moon''s uneven speed with a system of sliding pins. Because no comparable instrument survives from antiquity, researchers disagree on where the design was first made and how widely such devices were used. What is certain is that ancient Greek engineers combined astronomical theory and geared machinery far earlier than modern textbooks long assumed.','NONE','["They were kept secret by sailors.","Few if any comparable instruments have reached the present day.","They were built only for athletic games.","Ancient engineers rejected the design."]','B',1,'The passage notes that no comparable instrument survives, so few if any have reached us.',41),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, what does the fungus obtain from the plant?','Most land plants grow alongside fungi whose thread-like hyphae spread through the soil and connect to roots, forming what researchers call a mycorrhizal network. The exchange is mutual: the fungus, which cannot photosynthesise, receives sugars from the plant, while its fine threads gather water and minerals that the plant''s own roots could not reach alone. Through a single network several plants can be linked at once, and experiments with tagged carbon have shown that sugars and chemical warning signals pass between them, sometimes from older shaded trees toward younger ones in bright gaps. Whether this amounts to genuine cooperation or merely to shared pathways is contested; the fungi serve their own growth, and a network can equally move resources toward the plants that feed them most. What is not in doubt is that forest soils are threaded with living connections that bind individual plants into one functioning system.','NONE','["Water drawn from the roots","Minerals taken from the soil","Sugars that the plant produces","Chemical warning signals"]','C',1,'The fungus cannot photosynthesise and receives sugars from the plant in the exchange.',42),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "contested" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Most land plants grow alongside fungi whose thread-like hyphae spread through the soil and connect to roots, forming what researchers call a mycorrhizal network. The exchange is mutual: the fungus, which cannot photosynthesise, receives sugars from the plant, while its fine threads gather water and minerals that the plant''s own roots could not reach alone. Through a single network several plants can be linked at once, and experiments with tagged carbon have shown that sugars and chemical warning signals pass between them, sometimes from older shaded trees toward younger ones in bright gaps. Whether this amounts to genuine cooperation or merely to shared pathways is contested; the fungi serve their own growth, and a network can equally move resources toward the plants that feed them most. What is not in doubt is that forest soils are threaded with living connections that bind individual plants into one functioning system.','NONE','["agreed","proven","hidden","disputed"]','D',1,'Researchers disagree over the interpretation, so "contested" means disputed.',43),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about the benefit to plants?','Most land plants grow alongside fungi whose thread-like hyphae spread through the soil and connect to roots, forming what researchers call a mycorrhizal network. The exchange is mutual: the fungus, which cannot photosynthesise, receives sugars from the plant, while its fine threads gather water and minerals that the plant''s own roots could not reach alone. Through a single network several plants can be linked at once, and experiments with tagged carbon have shown that sugars and chemical warning signals pass between them, sometimes from older shaded trees toward younger ones in bright gaps. Whether this amounts to genuine cooperation or merely to shared pathways is contested; the fungi serve their own growth, and a network can equally move resources toward the plants that feed them most. What is not in doubt is that forest soils are threaded with living connections that bind individual plants into one functioning system.','NONE','["It is not always equal, since fungi favour the plants that feed them most.","It exists only in bright gaps.","It replaces photosynthesis entirely.","It has never been measured."]','A',1,'Because the fungi can route resources toward better-feeding plants, the benefit to each plant is not always equal.',44),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, why was the high-wheeler considered dangerous?','Before the 1880s the common bicycle was the high wheeler, whose huge front wheel gave speed but placed the rider far above the ground, so a stone or a rut could throw the rider head first over the handlebars in a feared accident called a header. The machine that replaced it was nicknamed the safety bicycle because its two equal wheels, diamond frame, and chain drive kept the rider seated low and above the rear wheel, and rubber pneumatic tyres after 1888 smoothed the ride further. Crucially, pedalling turned a rear wheel through gears rather than driving the front directly, so the frame could be built strong and light. Cheaper production after 1890 brought the price within reach of ordinary workers, and cycling clubs spread rapidly. Historians credit the safety bicycle with more personal independence for women and city workers than almost any earlier machine, since for the first time affordable travel did not depend on a horse or a paid fare.','NONE','["Its tyres burst easily.","A rider could be thrown forward over the handlebars.","It needed a horse to pull it.","Its chain broke on hills."]','B',1,'The passage says a stone or rut could throw the rider head first over the handlebars.',45),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The phrase "credit... with" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Before the 1880s the common bicycle was the high wheeler, whose huge front wheel gave speed but placed the rider far above the ground, so a stone or a rut could throw the rider head first over the handlebars in a feared accident called a header. The machine that replaced it was nicknamed the safety bicycle because its two equal wheels, diamond frame, and chain drive kept the rider seated low and above the rear wheel, and rubber pneumatic tyres after 1888 smoothed the ride further. Crucially, pedalling turned a rear wheel through gears rather than driving the front directly, so the frame could be built strong and light. Cheaper production after 1890 brought the price within reach of ordinary workers, and cycling clubs spread rapidly. Historians credit the safety bicycle with more personal independence for women and city workers than almost any earlier machine, since for the first time affordable travel did not depend on a horse or a paid fare.','NONE','["blame for","charge with","regard as the cause of","replace with"]','C',1,'Historians treat the safety bicycle as the cause of new independence, so "credit with" means regard as the cause of.',46),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about cycling before 1890?','Before the 1880s the common bicycle was the high wheeler, whose huge front wheel gave speed but placed the rider far above the ground, so a stone or a rut could throw the rider head first over the handlebars in a feared accident called a header. The machine that replaced it was nicknamed the safety bicycle because its two equal wheels, diamond frame, and chain drive kept the rider seated low and above the rear wheel, and rubber pneumatic tyres after 1888 smoothed the ride further. Crucially, pedalling turned a rear wheel through gears rather than driving the front directly, so the frame could be built strong and light. Cheaper production after 1890 brought the price within reach of ordinary workers, and cycling clubs spread rapidly. Historians credit the safety bicycle with more personal independence for women and city workers than almost any earlier machine, since for the first time affordable travel did not depend on a horse or a paid fare.','NONE','["Its tyres were already pneumatic.","It was cheaper than horse travel.","It already gave full independence.","It was mainly affordable to the wealthy."]','D',1,'Since only after 1890 did the price fall within reach of ordinary workers, before then cycling was mainly affordable to the wealthy.',47),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly explain?','For most of history people could name the mountains on land but not the shape of the ocean floor beneath their ships. The instrument that changed this is echo sounding, or sonar: a vessel sends a pulse of sound downward, times how long the echo takes to return, and converts that interval into depth using the known speed of sound in water. Early measurements came one at a time along a ship''s track, mapping a single narrow line. Modern surveys instead tow a fan of many beams that together image a wide swath of seabed, and satellites estimate depth from tiny bumps in the sea surface that betray the mass below. Repeated sounding has revealed a global system of mid-ocean ridges, deep trenches, and flat sediment plains, evidence that helped geologists confirm that the seafloor spreads. Even now, high-resolution maps cover only a fraction of the ocean, so most of the planet''s largest landscape remains surveyed only coarsely.','NONE','["How sound is used to measure the shape of the seafloor and what it revealed.","Why satellites sank beneath the ocean.","How ships avoid deep ocean trenches.","The cost of deep-sea drilling."]','A',1,'The passage describes echo sounding and the seabed features it has revealed.',48),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "betrays" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','For most of history people could name the mountains on land but not the shape of the ocean floor beneath their ships. The instrument that changed this is echo sounding, or sonar: a vessel sends a pulse of sound downward, times how long the echo takes to return, and converts that interval into depth using the known speed of sound in water. Early measurements came one at a time along a ship''s track, mapping a single narrow line. Modern surveys instead tow a fan of many beams that together image a wide swath of seabed, and satellites estimate depth from tiny bumps in the sea surface that betray the mass below. Repeated sounding has revealed a global system of mid-ocean ridges, deep trenches, and flat sediment plains, evidence that helped geologists confirm that the seafloor spreads. Even now, high-resolution maps cover only a fraction of the ocean, so most of the planet''s largest landscape remains surveyed only coarsely.','NONE','["hides","reveals","damages","creates"]','B',1,'The surface bumps give evidence of the mass below, so "betrays" means reveals.',49),
(@set06_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about ocean mapping today?','For most of history people could name the mountains on land but not the shape of the ocean floor beneath their ships. The instrument that changed this is echo sounding, or sonar: a vessel sends a pulse of sound downward, times how long the echo takes to return, and converts that interval into depth using the known speed of sound in water. Early measurements came one at a time along a ship''s track, mapping a single narrow line. Modern surveys instead tow a fan of many beams that together image a wide swath of seabed, and satellites estimate depth from tiny bumps in the sea surface that betray the mass below. Repeated sounding has revealed a global system of mid-ocean ridges, deep trenches, and flat sediment plains, evidence that helped geologists confirm that the seafloor spreads. Even now, high-resolution maps cover only a fraction of the ocean, so most of the planet''s largest landscape remains surveyed only coarsely.','NONE','["It is complete for most of the seabed.","It no longer requires ships.","Large areas are still mapped only roughly.","It has confirmed nothing about ridges."]','C',1,'The passage says high-resolution maps cover only a fraction of the ocean, so much of it is still mapped only roughly.',50);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Thanks for stopping by our booth at the career fair.','TTS','["I enjoyed learning about your internships.","You are welcome.","It does not matter.","See you last week."]','A',1,'A polite reply to a thank-you is to say you enjoyed learning about the company, which is the first option.',51),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Could you hand me the wrench on the bench?','TTS','["Yes, I could.","Sure, here you go.","It was really wrenching.","No, the bench is busy."]','B',1,'The speaker asks for a tool, so handing it over with the second reply fits best.',52),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','I finished my draft two days early for the writing centre.','TTS','["You must start it now.","Writing is always hard.","That leaves the tutors time to help.","Two days is a whole week."]','C',1,'Finishing early is best met with the reply that it gives the tutors time to help.',53),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Do you want to run the scene once more before rehearsal?','TTS','["I run every morning.","Once is never enough.","The scene was too long.","Let us take five and go again."]','D',1,'The suggestion to run the scene again is answered by agreeing to rehearse once more after a break.',54),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Sorry, I did not catch your name at the ticket office.','TTS','["It is Mei, M-E-I.","The name is on the door.","Tickets cost ten.","You already caught it."]','A',1,'When someone missed your name, the fitting reply is to repeat and spell it, the first option.',55),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','The exchange briefing was moved to Room 204 at ten.','TTS','["Room four is larger.","Thanks, I will head there now.","It is closed anyway.","Ten is too late."]','B',1,'News about a new room and time is met with thanks and going there, the second option.',56),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Would you rather start with the string section at rehearsal?','TTS','["The string is tied.","I mostly play guitar.","Fine by me either way.","Not at all today."]','C',1,'Asked to choose which section starts, the appropriate reply says either order is fine.',57),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','I was hoping you could proofread my statement of purpose.','TTS','["You hope far too much.","The purpose is clear now.","I have no time to write.","Send it over and I will look."]','D',1,'The request to proofread is answered by agreeing to read the document, the last option.',58),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','How long have you been playing the cello?','TTS','["About four years now.","Yes, I play quite often.","It is in the music room.","That is a nice cello."]','A',1,'A how-long question calls for a duration, so the first reply is correct.',59),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Can we swap our lab reports so I can check yours first?','TTS','["My report is quite long.","No problem, trade you.","The lab is closed now.","You should check it first."]','B',1,'A request to swap reports is accepted with the second reply agreeing to trade.',60),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','The career fair starts before my usual lecture ends.','TTS','["You could just join the second half.","That fair is very far.","Lectures are always long.","You must skip the class."]','A',1,'The helpful response to a timing clash suggests attending the later part of the fair.',61),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Have you picked your courses for the exchange yet?','TTS','["Yes, courses do exist.","An exchange of ideas is nice.","You should pick them later.","Not yet, I am still deciding."]','D',1,'A have-you-yet question is answered by saying you are still deciding, the last option.',62),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Could you keep the noise down during my online interview?','TTS','["Sorry, I will turn it off right now.","Your interview is online.","I actually like the noise.","You should keep it up."]','A',1,'A request to lower noise is answered by apologising and stopping it, the first option.',63),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Which line should I stand in at the ticket office?','TTS','["Lines are always round.","The left one is faster today.","I already bought two.","You can stand whenever."]','B',1,'Asked to choose a queue, the reply points to the faster line, the second option.',64),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','I am nervous about speaking at the briefing.','TTS','["Briefings are usually short.","You can speak a little later.","You will do great, just go slowly.","Why speak in public at all?"]','C',1,'Confession of nervousness is best met with reassurance and advice to pace, the third option.',65),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Did the physics lab run out of sensor kits?','TTS','["The sensors are quite new.","Physics can be hard.","A kit is just a box.","No, there were plenty left."]','D',1,'A did-it-run-out question is answered by saying plenty remained, the last option.',66),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Thanks for covering my shift at the venue.','TTS','["Anytime, you did the same last week.","You are welcome to sit down.","That was my shift anyway.","You should cover it now."]','A',1,'The right response to thanks is to say it was no trouble and note the favour was returned.',67),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the sentence and choose the best response.','Should we meet at the writing centre at four or five?','TTS','["The centre is open late.","Four works better for me.","Let us write at noon.","Meet somewhere else instead."]','B',1,'Asked to pick between four and five, the reply chooses one time, the second option.',68),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Hi, I saw your company''s booth at the career fair and I am interested in the summer internship. — Woman: Great, the application closes Friday; do you have questions about the selection process? — Student: Yes, is the coding test the first step? — Woman: Actually we do a phone screen first, and only then a coding test, so prepare to talk about your projects.','TTS','["To ask about a summer internship","To find a job that day","To return an application form","To cancel a coding test"]','A',1,'The student says he wants information about the internship, so he visits the booth to ask about it.',69),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Hi, I saw your company''s booth at the career fair and I am interested in the summer internship. — Woman: Great, the application closes Friday; do you have questions about the selection process? — Student: Yes, is the coding test the first step? — Woman: Actually we do a phone screen first, and only then a coding test, so prepare to talk about your projects.','TTS','["A coding test","A phone screen","A written essay","An office visit"]','B',1,'The recruiter states that a phone screen comes before the coding test.',70),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Hi, I saw your company''s booth at the career fair and I am interested in the summer internship. — Woman: Great, the application closes Friday; do you have questions about the selection process? — Student: Yes, is the coding test the first step? — Woman: Actually we do a phone screen first, and only then a coding test, so prepare to talk about your projects.','TTS','["On Monday","On Wednesday","On Friday","Next week"]','C',1,'The woman says the application closes Friday.',71),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I got some strange numbers from the sensor in the physics lab. — Man: Did you calibrate it before the run? — Student: I thought you had done it. — Man: Neither of us calibrated it, so those readings are useless; let us redo the measurement and write down each step this time.','TTS','["The sensor gave strange readings","The lab had been locked","The numbers were missing","His partner had left"]','A',1,'The student opens by reporting odd readings from the sensor.',72),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I got some strange numbers from the sensor in the physics lab. — Man: Did you calibrate it before the run? — Student: I thought you had done it. — Man: Neither of us calibrated it, so those readings are useless; let us redo the measurement and write down each step this time.','TTS','["The lab was too cold","The sensor was never calibrated","The battery had died","The report was submitted late"]','B',1,'The man explains that neither partner calibrated the sensor, so the readings cannot be trusted.',73),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I got some strange numbers from the sensor in the physics lab. — Man: Did you calibrate it before the run? — Student: I thought you had done it. — Man: Neither of us calibrated it, so those readings are useless; let us redo the measurement and write down each step this time.','TTS','["Submit the original results","Ask a different lab","Skip the assignment","Redo the measurement carefully"]','D',1,'The man proposes to redo the measurement and record each step, so they will repeat it carefully.',74),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Welcome to the ticket office, how can I help you? — Man: Do you still have one seat for tonight''s play? — Woman: We have two left in the front row, but they sit very close to the stage. — Man: That is fine, I am rather short anyway, so I will take one.','TTS','["A seat for tonight''s performance","A refund for a ticket","Two front-row seats","A poster of the play"]','A',1,'The man asks whether one seat remains for tonight''s show.',75),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Welcome to the ticket office, how can I help you? — Man: Do you still have one seat for tonight''s play? — Woman: We have two left in the front row, but they sit very close to the stage. — Man: That is fine, I am rather short anyway, so I will take one.','TTS','["They are all sold out","They are in the balcony","They are in the front row near the stage","They are offered for free"]','C',1,'The woman says the two remaining seats are in the front row close to the stage.',76),
