(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention cast members: tonight''s music rehearsal will start thirty minutes late because the piano is being tuned, so we will begin with the vocal warm-up and move to the accompaniment once the tuner leaves. Please bring your updated scores.','TTS','["A change to the rehearsal schedule","The sale of a piano","A cancelled concert","A new lead actor"]','A',1,'The speaker explains a late start and a revised order, which is a change to the rehearsal schedule.',81),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention cast members: tonight''s music rehearsal will start thirty minutes late because the piano is being tuned, so we will begin with the vocal warm-up and move to the accompaniment once the tuner leaves. Please bring your updated scores.','TTS','["Play the accompaniment","Tune the piano","Do a vocal warm-up","Leave early"]','C',1,'Since the piano is occupied, the group begins with the vocal warm-up.',82),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention cast members: tonight''s music rehearsal will start thirty minutes late because the piano is being tuned, so we will begin with the vocal warm-up and move to the accompaniment once the tuner leaves. Please bring your updated scores.','TTS','["Scores from last term","Their updated scores","A spare piano","A packed lunch"]','B',1,'The closing line asks everyone to bring their updated scores.',83),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Thank you for waiting at the ticket office. Due to a lighting fault in the main hall, tonight''s performance has moved to the studio theatre, and every previously bought ticket will be honoured there without paying again. Doors open twenty minutes before the start.','TTS','["There is a lighting fault in the main hall","An actor has fallen ill","The office is being closed","The show has sold out"]','A',1,'The announcement names a lighting fault in the main hall as the reason for moving.',84),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Thank you for waiting at the ticket office. Due to a lighting fault in the main hall, tonight''s performance has moved to the studio theatre, and every previously bought ticket will be honoured there without paying again. Doors open twenty minutes before the start.','TTS','["They become worthless","They must be bought again","They are refunded","They remain valid at the new venue"]','D',1,'The speaker says old tickets will be honoured without paying again, so they stay valid at the new venue.',85),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Thank you for waiting at the ticket office. Due to a lighting fault in the main hall, tonight''s performance has moved to the studio theatre, and every previously bought ticket will be honoured there without paying again. Doors open twenty minutes before the start.','TTS','["One hour early","After the start","Twenty minutes before the start","At noon"]','C',1,'The final sentence states that the doors open twenty minutes before the start.',86),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','A reminder for the exchange programme field trip: please meet at the west gate at seven rather than eight, because border checks take longer at the weekend, and hand your host family contact form to a group leader before you board the bus.','TTS','["Students on an exchange trip","Ticket office staff","Physics lab partners","The theatre cast"]','A',1,'The opening line addresses the exchange programme field trip, so it is for students on that trip.',87),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','A reminder for the exchange programme field trip: please meet at the west gate at seven rather than eight, because border checks take longer at the weekend, and hand your host family contact form to a group leader before you board the bus.','TTS','["The bus leaves earlier","Border checks are slower at the weekend","The gate is very far","It is a public holiday"]','B',1,'The meeting time moves to seven because border checks take longer at the weekend.',88),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','A reminder for the exchange programme field trip: please meet at the west gate at seven rather than eight, because border checks take longer at the weekend, and hand your host family contact form to a group leader before you board the bus.','TTS','["Your passport","A route map","The bus fare","The host family contact form"]','D',1,'The speaker says to hand the host family contact form to a group leader before boarding.',89),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','The field I want to describe today is underwater archaeology, the study of ships and settlements now lying beneath the sea. Sites become flooded for two reasons: a vessel sinks, or land that people once lived on is drowned as sea levels rise. Water preserves wood and cloth far better than dry soil does, because cold deep water slows the bacteria and fungi that would rot organic material, so archaeologists recover ropes, baskets and even food that vanish on a land site. The difficulty is not the digging but the recording: divers cannot easily write, so every find is photographed on a fixed grid and its coordinates logged before anything is lifted. Once an object is raised and begins to dry, it can crack or shrink, which is why timbers are often supported in tanks of cold water for years while conservators slowly replace the water inside them with a stabilising resin. It is slow, patient work, and much of it happens above the surface.','TTS','["How underwater sites form and how finds are conserved","Why ships sink at sea","The history of diving suits","How to photograph a wreck"]','A',1,'The talk explains how sites flood and why finds must be recorded and conserved, so it is about underwater sites and their preservation.',90),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','The field I want to describe today is underwater archaeology, the study of ships and settlements now lying beneath the sea. Sites become flooded for two reasons: a vessel sinks, or land that people once lived on is drowned as sea levels rise. Water preserves wood and cloth far better than dry soil does, because cold deep water slows the bacteria and fungi that would rot organic material, so archaeologists recover ropes, baskets and even food that vanish on a land site. The difficulty is not the digging but the recording: divers cannot easily write, so every find is photographed on a fixed grid and its coordinates logged before anything is lifted. Once an object is raised and begins to dry, it can crack or shrink, which is why timbers are often supported in tanks of cold water for years while conservators slowly replace the water inside them with a stabilising resin. It is slow, patient work, and much of it happens above the surface.','TTS','["Sunlight reaches the wreck","Salt hardens the wood","Cold deep water slows decay organisms","Fish clean the site"]','C',1,'The professor says cold deep water slows the bacteria and fungi that cause decay, which is why organics survive.',91),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','The field I want to describe today is underwater archaeology, the study of ships and settlements now lying beneath the sea. Sites become flooded for two reasons: a vessel sinks, or land that people once lived on is drowned as sea levels rise. Water preserves wood and cloth far better than dry soil does, because cold deep water slows the bacteria and fungi that would rot organic material, so archaeologists recover ropes, baskets and even food that vanish on a land site. The difficulty is not the digging but the recording: divers cannot easily write, so every find is photographed on a fixed grid and its coordinates logged before anything is lifted. Once an object is raised and begins to dry, it can crack or shrink, which is why timbers are often supported in tanks of cold water for years while conservators slowly replace the water inside them with a stabilising resin. It is slow, patient work, and much of it happens above the surface.','TTS','["They sketch it in a notebook","They photograph it on a fixed grid and log its coordinates","They write a number on the object","They move it before recording"]','B',1,'The talk states that each find is photographed on a fixed grid and its coordinates logged before it is lifted.',92),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','The field I want to describe today is underwater archaeology, the study of ships and settlements now lying beneath the sea. Sites become flooded for two reasons: a vessel sinks, or land that people once lived on is drowned as sea levels rise. Water preserves wood and cloth far better than dry soil does, because cold deep water slows the bacteria and fungi that would rot organic material, so archaeologists recover ropes, baskets and even food that vanish on a land site. The difficulty is not the digging but the recording: divers cannot easily write, so every find is photographed on a fixed grid and its coordinates logged before anything is lifted. Once an object is raised and begins to dry, it can crack or shrink, which is why timbers are often supported in tanks of cold water for years while conservators slowly replace the water inside them with a stabilising resin. It is slow, patient work, and much of it happens above the surface.','TTS','["It can safely be left in open air","It must be dried as quickly as possible","It never changes once lifted","It can be damaged unless supported and treated"]','D',1,'Because drying timber makes it crack or shrink, raising it requires support and slow treatment to avoid damage.',93),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Let us turn from the buildings themselves to the roads between them, because the history of transport networks often decides where cities grow. A route becomes valuable when many independent users need the same link, and each added user makes that link more useful still, a pattern economists describe as a network effect. Railways show it clearly: a single line to one mine barely pays, but connect that mine to two towns and a port, and freight and passengers arrive who never would have used one isolated track. Planners exploit this by siting stations where several lines can cross, knowing that a hub pulls housing and shops toward it, which in turn justifies extending the network further outward. The same logic now shapes decisions about high-speed rail and even cycle-lane networks: the question is less whether a single stretch is busy today than whether it will complete a connection that makes many other trips possible.','TTS','["Why transport links gain value as connections grow","The cost of opening mines","The interior design of train stations","How to price a season ticket"]','A',1,'The talk is built around the network effect and why links become valuable as more of them connect, so it is about transport links gaining value with connections.',94),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Let us turn from the buildings themselves to the roads between them, because the history of transport networks often decides where cities grow. A route becomes valuable when many independent users need the same link, and each added user makes that link more useful still, a pattern economists describe as a network effect. Railways show it clearly: a single line to one mine barely pays, but connect that mine to two towns and a port, and freight and passengers arrive who never would have used one isolated track. Planners exploit this by siting stations where several lines can cross, knowing that a hub pulls housing and shops toward it, which in turn justifies extending the network further outward. The same logic now shapes decisions about high-speed rail and even cycle-lane networks: the question is less whether a single stretch is busy today than whether it will complete a connection that makes many other trips possible.','TTS','["Fares fall whenever lines run long","A link grows more useful as more users need it","Cities tend to avoid railways","Every separate track pays for itself"]','B',1,'The professor defines the network effect as a link becoming more useful as each added user needs it.',95),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Let us turn from the buildings themselves to the roads between them, because the history of transport networks often decides where cities grow. A route becomes valuable when many independent users need the same link, and each added user makes that link more useful still, a pattern economists describe as a network effect. Railways show it clearly: a single line to one mine barely pays, but connect that mine to two towns and a port, and freight and passengers arrive who never would have used one isolated track. Planners exploit this by siting stations where several lines can cross, knowing that a hub pulls housing and shops toward it, which in turn justifies extending the network further outward. The same logic now shapes decisions about high-speed rail and even cycle-lane networks: the question is less whether a single stretch is busy today than whether it will complete a connection that makes many other trips possible.','TTS','["Because land is cheaper there","Because trains are slower there","Because a hub attracts housing and shops and justifies more lines","Because tunnels are easier there"]','C',1,'The talk says planners place stations where lines cross because a hub draws housing and shops and justifies further lines.',96),
(@set06_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Let us turn from the buildings themselves to the roads between them, because the history of transport networks often decides where cities grow. A route becomes valuable when many independent users need the same link, and each added user makes that link more useful still, a pattern economists describe as a network effect. Railways show it clearly: a single line to one mine barely pays, but connect that mine to two towns and a port, and freight and passengers arrive who never would have used one isolated track. Planners exploit this by siting stations where several lines can cross, knowing that a hub pulls housing and shops toward it, which in turn justifies extending the network further outward. The same logic now shapes decisions about high-speed rail and even cycle-lane networks: the question is less whether a single stretch is busy today than whether it will complete a connection that makes many other trips possible.','TTS','["Judge a route only by its current traffic","Copy older cities without change","Avoid building hubs at all","Weigh whether a stretch completes useful future connections"]','D',1,'The closing line says the real question is whether a stretch completes a connection enabling other trips, not its traffic today.',97);
